{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261027T231204Z-a0e7ec5c", "ts": "2026-10-27T23:15:26Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.2", "branch": "feature/1078-unloaded-tripwire"}, "body": {"base_commit": "eb4de60ea330e0d9cfc8e1a40665936e76b2d2ad", "base_tree": "d28058350bc526e7fcfae56e34065884436ebebd", "head_tree": "d1f01c73670f9bf9e1f878e3dea64e92dbaa553d", "head_commit": "c8c55ae3de97c7db9c7230b6225dbeb5f5a058d5", "dispatch_commit": "c8c55ae3de97c7db9c7230b6225dbeb5f5a058d5", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "fallback-no-prior-findings", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["README.md", "documentation/MIGRATION.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/anchor_archive.py", "plugin/lib/anchor_repair.py", "plugin/lib/init_product.py", "plugin/lib/install_reference_probes.py", "plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py", "plugin/lib/plugin_activation.py", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "tests/test_anchor_repair.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_plugin_migrate.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-unloaded-tripwire.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/operational-spec.md", ".prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md", "README.md", "documentation/MIGRATION.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/anchor_archive.py", "plugin/lib/anchor_repair.py", "plugin/lib/init_product.py", "plugin/lib/install_reference_probes.py", "plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py", "plugin/lib/plugin_activation.py", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "tests/test_anchor_repair.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_plugin_migrate.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-unloaded-tripwire.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/operational-spec.md", ".prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "init-product reports .claude/settings.json edited when a malformed hooks shape means nothing was written", "recommendation": "Instance. Cause: _install_reference_present now requires tripwire_state == PRESENT, and a MALFORMED hooks shape can never reach PRESENT. So a repo whose settings already carry the full install reference but whose hooks.SessionStart is not a list gets '.claude/settings.json' in `edited` on every first scaffold, while transform_settings writes nothing (ensure_tripwire refuses the shape and the reference is unchanged). The skipped_warning that goes with it is correct, so the operator is told the hook was not written. Only the edit list is wrong, and only once, since already_scaffolded short-circuits after that. A fix, if wanted: treat MALFORMED like PRESENT in _install_reference_present, since skipped_warning already reports it. No edit is needed for this to merge.", "files": ["plugin/lib/init_product.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "install-tripwire --apply rewrites an owner-formatted settings.json whole (indent, CRLF, non-ASCII escaped)", "recommendation": "Instance, and a decision the docstring records ('line endings are not [preserved], since the settings writer prawduct already ships writes \\n JSON'). The learnings rule 'Preserve line endings in any writer editing a file it didn't create' bears on it. Doctor's repair is the first writer that edits an existing owner-maintained settings.json after onboarding, and json.dumps(indent=2) with the default ensure_ascii also turns any non-ASCII text into \\u escapes. The result is a whole-file diff in a committed file around a one-hook change. The meaning is unchanged, so this is cosmetic. Accept on the record, or pass ensure_ascii=False and keep the original newline style in install() if the churn turns out to matter.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "read_settings_state has no caller", "recommendation": "unloaded_tripwire.read_settings_state is defined, but a grep of plugin/, tests/, documentation/ and .prawduct/artifacts/ finds no caller. Every reader uses read_settings() or tripwire_state(), and that matches what the module docstring names as the reader path. It is a two-line helper, so the cost is small: delete it, or leave it if you want it as public API. No other edit is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Taking PLUGIN_ID from migrate_plugin forces two lazy imports going the other way", "recommendation": "unloaded_tripwire imports PLUGIN_ID from migrate_plugin at top level. That forces migrate_plugin to import unloaded_tripwire lazily, in both transform_settings and _note_skipped_tripwire, each with a comment explaining the cycle. plugin_activation already defines the same PLUGIN_ID literal, imports nothing from lib/, and is the module the tripwire's verdict mirrors. Importing it from there would remove the cycle, but would lean on the duplicated literal instead of INSTALL_REFERENCE, which is the authority (test_the_plugin_id_is_the_install_contracts pins agreement either way). The current layout is a defensible choice and is documented. Recorded so whoever next touches PLUGIN_ID's three homes knows the cycle depends on this import.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "_install_reference_present now also checks the tripwire, so a malformed hooks shape reports a settings edit that never happens", "recommendation": "init_product._install_reference_present now also requires tripwire_state == PRESENT, so its name and the 'install reference' it promises no longer match. In the MALFORMED-hooks case it returns False on every run. init-product then lists .claude/settings.json as edited, and transform_settings is invoked, but ensure_tripwire refuses and nothing changes. The skipped_warning line does tell the owner the real story. Consider renaming it (for example _settings_current) or excluding MALFORMED from the edit report. The settings file is also parsed three times on this path (here, in transform_settings, in skipped_warning), each time with slightly different rules for invalid JSON. That is tolerable, but it is the place a fourth policy would creep in.", "files": ["plugin/lib/init_product.py", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner: two added since the last review's list", "recommendation": "Scope: none. priors: R-10 (three unplanned decisions: install-tripwire subcommand, tripwire-missing advisory, session-banner SUBSTANCE row) and R-9 (inferred #1078 item-3 scope, --plugin-dir false warning) were accepted for the PR description and are not re-raised. Two more were settled by review fixes in c8c55ae3 and belong in the same owner list. (1) R-1's worktree rule widens plugin_activation itself: check-plugin-active in a linked worktree of a recorded checkout now reports `active`, where doctor used to report a stale install record. That changes an existing command's verdict, not only the new hook's. (2) Opt-out semantics: re-running /prawduct:onboard or /prawduct:migrate never adds the hook back, and only doctor's offered `install-tripwire --apply` does. The plan's 'Decisions made mid-build' records both, so only the owner's confirm-or-correct is missing. Alignment-pass miss: none seen.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plugin_activation.py", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Design Intent #5's body edited to bless the tripwire, beside a Direction decision that leaves its own clause as ratified", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The rule 'Editing a norm's STATEMENT in the same commit as the code it would bless is the amend-to-match-own-code tell' applies to architecture.md Design Intent #5 ('what we want to be true'). Its 'Governance code and methodology ship in the plugin and stay there' gained 'with one exception: the unloaded-plugin tripwire ...' in the same commit as the hook. The Direction-norm Decision of 2026-10-27, builder-drafted for the owner's veto, names exactly that sentence as the thing departed from, and it leaves the Direction clause's wording alone. The two surfaces handle one departure differently. If the owner vetoes the Decision, the Design Intent text still reads as settled intent. Cheapest consistent shape: keep the Design Intent statement as it was and point to the 2026-10-27 Decision, or put the owner's confirmation of both in the PR's owner list. This is an oracle record and clears no Records Pass bar on its own; it is raised under the Learnings Cross-Check.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "install-tripwire --json is produced with no named consumer", "recommendation": "Scope: class (repair commands whose --json the api-contract marks 'no JSON consumer today': reanchor, stale-plugin-caches, now install-tripwire). The rule 'A channel that is produced and never consumed is a DEFECT' applies. Doctor relays the human form, and only a test reads the JSON. The api-contract states this openly and the command follows its siblings' convention, so this is a first instance of the class for this branch, not a new defect. No edit is wanted here. Whether that convention earns its keep is a repo-level question.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: no finding escalates; one oracle sentence superseded beside its correction", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read list (learnings-files --for-diff): core.md, authoring.md, gates.md, hook-surface.md, release.md, tests.md. Rules checked against this change: the defense-reachability rule ('When defense-in-depth is the reason a risk needn't be verified, check the defense is REACHABLE') holds. The anchor fallback for hosts where project hooks don't run (Windows without Git Bash) loads through CLAUDE.md -> AGENTS.md with no plugin. The one-owner / mirrored-rule duplication (plugin_activation vs. the inlined verdict) is pinned by test_verdict_agrees_with_plugin_activation, including the worktree fixture. 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees ... delete superseded sentences': the build plan's mid-build bullet still says the hook is 'found by a leading `: prawduct-unloaded-tripwire v1` no-op line and replaced in place when stale'. The R-6/8/11 bullet below it, and the code, say the tag has no version and a stale copy is removed and the current group appended. The plan is an internal oracle record, the correction sits two bullets down, and nothing reads the stale sentence, so no edit is wanted. No rules were added or changed this cycle.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-unloaded-tripwire.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: #1078 already dispositioned; C-B3 open items in the touched anchor area (#928, #384)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. priors: R-13 (#1078 closes at merge via the fragment's closes:) and R-14 (#797) were accepted and are not re-raised. `cache-query affecting` over the changed lib/skill/hook files returns brookstalley/prawduct#928 (portable host adapters, Codex first). This branch rewrites the governance anchor, which every host reads, and its loaded-signal line is now Claude Code-specific ('loaded if its session banner appeared'), so assess #928 for this change. #384 (the SessionStart banner has no provenance marker) is related: the anchor now makes the banner the loaded signal, which raises the value of #384 without resolving it. #710 (onboard can look onboarded with governance never active) is already shipped. No other open item appears resolved. Cache confirmed 2026-10-27T21:15Z.", "files": ["plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Records Pass: five oracle files covered, none clears a bar", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Covered: .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md, .prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md, .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-unloaded-tripwire.md, .prawduct/artifacts/operational-spec.md, .prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md. Checked against code: install-tripwire's exit codes and JSON keys (api-contract), ANCHOR_V6 archived and the 'session banner' SUBSTANCE row (change record), the opt-out claim that onboard/migrate are no-ops once `distribution: plugin` is recorded (init_product.already_scaffolded, migrate_plugin.already_migrated), a stale copy removed and the current group appended (ensure_tripwire), the matcher pinned to the banner's, and the per-clone advisory dismissal (tested). No shipping falsehood, no record that misleads into action, nothing merge-stopping. Below-bar items are named in the Learnings Cross-Check note and want no edit.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md"], "fid": "R-11"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 0, "note": 11}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "unloaded-tripwire",